    "story": "He is the last of them who can still be asked. The regiment's memory now lives behind one white beard, in a mind that keeps its own ledger — generous with the funny stories, exact about the weather on certain mornings, and silent, always, at the same three names. Historians write to him. He answers in pencil, a page at a time, and what he leaves out is not forgetfulness. It is command. Some of the past he intends to take with him, and it can wait its turn.",
    "notes": "The set closes on its most direct exchange: the old veteran faces the lens nearly frontally, his gaze level into the camera, the great beard spread as the frame's central field of texture. Heim builds the depth with a single device — a comrade's shoulder, blurred large in the right foreground, so the viewer stands inside the ranks, looking over it. A flag's corner cuts the top edge; the ground is bleached tone. This is the sitting from which the color set's \"What It Cost\" also descends. For collectors: the valedictory portrait — one gaze, one beard, one shoulder between us and him.",
